Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $H$ be a complex Hilbert space ([[def-hilbert-space]]) with inner product linear in the first argument, let $(e_n)_{n\in\mathbb N}$, where $\mathbb N=\{0,1,2,\dots\}$, be a complete orthonormal family of $H$ ([[def-orthonormal-family-complete-orthonormal-system-and-hilbert-basis]]), and let $(\lambda_n)_{n\in\mathbb N}\subseteq\mathbb R$. Put

$$D_0:=\Bigl\{\sum_{n\in F}c_ne_n:\ F\subseteq\mathbb N\ \text{finite},\ c_n\in\mathbb C\Bigr\},\qquad S\Bigl(\sum_{n\in F}c_ne_n\Bigr):=\sum_{n\in F}\lambda_nc_ne_n,$$

and

$$D(A):=\Bigl\{x\in H:\sum_{n\in\mathbb N}\lambda_n^2\,|\langle x,e_n\rangle|^2<\infty\Bigr\},\qquad Ax:=\sum_{n\in\mathbb N}\lambda_n\langle x,e_n\rangle\,e_n .$$

Then:

(a) $S$ is a well-defined, densely defined linear operator with domain $D_0$ ([[def-unbounded-linear-operator-domain-and-graph]]), and it is symmetric ([[def-symmetric-self-adjoint-and-essentially-self-adjoint]]);

(b) the series defining $Ax$ converges in $H$ for every $x\in D(A)$, with $\|Ax\|^2=\sum_n\lambda_n^2|\langle x,e_n\rangle|^2$, so $A$ is a linear operator with domain $D(A)$ and $S\subseteq A$;

(c) $S^*=A$ ([[def-adjoint-of-a-densely-defined-unbounded-operator]]), hence $A$ is closed and $A^*=A$: $A$ is self-adjoint and $\overline S=A$, so $S$ is essentially self-adjoint and $A$ is its unique self-adjoint extension;

(d) $D_0$ is a core for $A$ ([[def-densely-defined-closed-and-closable-operator]]): the finite truncations $x_N:=\sum_{n=0}^N\langle x,e_n\rangle e_n\in D_0$ of $x\in D(A)$ satisfy $x_N\to x$ and $Ax_N\to Ax$ in $H$ as $N\to\infty$;

(e) $\langle Ax,x\rangle=\sum_n\lambda_n|\langle x,e_n\rangle|^2$ for $x\in D(A)$, so $\langle Ax,x\rangle$ is real and $\langle Ax,x\rangle\ge c\|x\|^2$ whenever $\lambda_n\ge c$ for all $n$; and $Ae_n=\lambda_ne_n$ for every $n$, so each $\lambda_n$ is an eigenvalue of $A$ with eigenvector $e_n$.

No assertion is made about a differential or multiplication realisation of $A$: the lemma concerns only a specified complete orthonormal family and real eigenvalue sequence.

Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $L^2=L^2(\mathbb R,dy)$ be the complex Lebesgue space with inner product linear in the first argument, and let $g_0,P,(g_n)_{n\in\mathbb N}$ and $H_n$ be as in [[thm-nrqm-oscillator-hermite-recurrence]]. Define $h:=fg_0\in L^1(\mathbb R)$ for $f\in L^2$ and write $\widehat h(\xi)=\int_{\mathbb R}h(y)e^{-2\pi i\xi y}\,dy$ for its $L^1$ Fourier transform. Then:

(a) (moment criterion) if $\int_{\mathbb R}f\,q\,g_0=0$ for every polynomial $q\in\mathbb C[y]$, then $f=0$ almost everywhere;

(b) the family $(g_n)_{n\in\mathbb N}$ is a complete orthonormal family, i.e. a Hilbert basis, of $L^2$ ([[def-orthonormal-family-complete-orthonormal-system-and-hilbert-basis]]): the only $f\in L^2$ with $\langle f,g_n\rangle=0$ for all $n$ is $f=0$, and consequently every $f\in L^2$ satisfies $f=\sum_n\langle f,g_n\rangle g_n$ in $L^2$ with $\|f\|^2=\sum_n|\langle f,g_n\rangle|^2$ ([[thm-parseval-equivalences-for-a-complete-orthonormal-family]]);

(c) the polynomial-Gaussian space $P$ is dense in $L^2$.

Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $H$ be a complex Hilbert space ([[def-hilbert-space]]) with inner product linear in the first argument, let $(e_n)_{n\in\mathbb N}$, $\mathbb N=\{0,1,2,\dots\}$, be a complete orthonormal family of $H$ ([[def-orthonormal-family-complete-orthonormal-system-and-hilbert-basis]]), and for $x\in H$ write $c_n:=\langle x,e_n\rangle$, so that $x=\sum_nc_ne_n$ and $\sum_n|c_n|^2=\|x\|^2$. Define

$$D(a):=\Bigl\{x:\sum_n n|c_n|^2<\infty\Bigr\},\quad ax:=\sum_{m\ge0}\sqrt{m+1}\,c_{m+1}\,e_m ;$$
$$D(a^\dagger):=\Bigl\{x:\sum_n(n+1)|c_n|^2<\infty\Bigr\},\quad a^\dagger x:=\sum_{m\ge1}\sqrt m\,c_{m-1}\,e_m ;$$
$$D(N):=\Bigl\{x:\sum_n n^2|c_n|^2<\infty\Bigr\},\quad Nx:=\sum_n n\,c_n\,e_n .$$

Then:

(a) $a,a^\dagger,N$ are well-defined linear operators with the displayed domains; these domains contain the algebraic span $D_0=\operatorname{span}\{e_n\}$ and are dense in $H$; and $\|ax\|^2=\sum_nn|c_n|^2$, $\|a^\dagger x\|^2=\sum_n(n+1)|c_n|^2$, $\|Nx\|^2=\sum_nn^2|c_n|^2$;

(b) $a$ and $a^\dagger$ are closed, densely defined operators with $a^*=a^\dagger$ and $(a^\dagger)^*=a$;

(c) $\ker a=\mathbb Ce_0$ and $\ker a^\dagger=\{0\}$;

(d) $D(aa^\dagger)=D(a^\dagger a)=D(N)$; on $D(N)$ one has $a^\dagger a=N$ and $aa^\dagger=N+I$, hence $[a,a^\dagger]x=aa^\dagger x-a^\dagger ax=x$ for every $x\in D(N)$; and $D(N)$ is exactly the set of $x\in H$ for which both $a(a^\dagger x)$ and $a^\dagger(ax)$ are defined;

(e) $N$ is self-adjoint with $Ne_n=ne_n$ and spectrum $\sigma(N)=\mathbb N$ ([[thm-nrqm-oscillator-diagonal-spectrum]]), and for $x\in D(N)$ the partial sums $x_M:=\sum_{n\le M}c_ne_n$ satisfy $x_M\to x$ and $Nx_M\to Nx$;

(f) if $H=L^2(\mathbb R,dy)$ with the Hermite orthonormal family $e_n=g_n$ of [[thm-nrqm-oscillator-hermite-recurrence]], then the operators $a_P,a^\dagger_P:P\to P$ of that theorem are the restrictions of $a,a^\dagger$ to $P$, and $a$ and $a^\dagger$ are exactly the closures of $a_P$ and $a^\dagger_P$.

Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $H=L^2(\mathbb R,dy)$ with inner product linear in the first argument, let $(g_n)_{n\in\mathbb N}$ be the Hermite family of [[thm-nrqm-oscillator-hermite-recurrence]], and let $a,a^\dagger,N$ be the closed ladder operators and number operator of [[lem-nrqm-oscillator-closed-ladder-domains]] with domains $D(a),D(a^\dagger),D(N)$ and $N=a^\dagger a$ on $D(N)$. For $z\in\mathbb C$ put

$$\psi_z:=e^{-|z|^2/2}\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{z^n}{\sqrt{n!}}\,g_n ,$$

the series being taken in $L^2$. Then:

(a) the series converges in $L^2$, $\|\psi_z\|=1$, and $\psi_z=e^{-|z|^2/2}\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{z^n}{\sqrt{n!}}g_n$ is the unique element of $L^2$ with coefficients $c_n=e^{-|z|^2/2}z^n/\sqrt{n!}$ in the basis $(g_n)$;

(b) $\psi_z\in D(N^k)$ for every $k\in\mathbb N$, where $N^k$ is the $k$-fold iterate of $N$ with domain $D(N^k)=\{x:\sum_nn^{2k}|\langle x,g_n\rangle|^2<\infty\}$; in particular

$$\sum_nn^{2k}|c_n|^2=e^{-|z|^2}\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{n^{2k}|z|^{2n}}{n!}<\infty;$$

(c) $a\psi_z=z\psi_z$ (so $\psi_z$ is a unit eigenvector of the annihilation operator with eigenvalue $z$), and $\|a\psi_z\|^2=|z|^2$;

(d) $\langle\psi_z,\psi_w\rangle=e^{-(|z|^2+|w|^2)/2+z\overline w}=e^{-|z-w|^2/2}e^{i\operatorname{Im}(z\overline w)}$ for all $z,w\in\mathbb C$, so $\|\psi_z-\psi_w\|^2=2\bigl(1-e^{-|z-w|^2/2}\cos(\operatorname{Im}(z\overline w))\bigr)$ and the map $z\mapsto\psi_z$ is injective;

(e) $\langle N\psi_z,\psi_z\rangle=|z|^2$ and $\|a^\dagger\psi_z\|^2=|z|^2+1$, and $\psi_0=g_0$.

The completeness of $(g_n)$ ([[thm-nrqm-oscillator-hermite-completeness]]) is used only to invoke the closed ladder operators of [[lem-nrqm-oscillator-closed-ladder-domains]] on a complete orthonormal family; the convergence, normalization and moment statements (a)-(e) then use the orthonormality of [[thm-nrqm-oscillator-hermite-orthogonality]] and those operator domains.

Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $(g_n)_{n\in\mathbb N}$ be the Hermite family of $L^2(\mathbb R,dy)$, $N=a^\dagger a$ the number operator of [[lem-nrqm-oscillator-closed-ladder-domains]], and for $z\in\mathbb C$ let $\psi_z=e^{-|z|^2/2}\sum_n\frac{z^n}{\sqrt{n!}}g_n$ be the coherent vector of [[thm-nrqm-oscillator-coherent-state-convergence]]. Define

$$p_n:=|\langle g_n,\psi_z\rangle|^2=e^{-|z|^2}\frac{|z|^{2n}}{n!}\qquad(n\in\mathbb N).$$

Then:

(a) $(p_n)_{n\in\mathbb N}$ is a probability distribution on $\mathbb N$: $p_n\ge0$ and $\sum_np_n=1$;

(b) $\langle N\rangle:=\langle N\psi_z,\psi_z\rangle=\sum_nn\,p_n=|z|^2$;

(c) $\operatorname{Var}(N):=\langle(N-|z|^2I)^2\psi_z,\psi_z\rangle=\sum_n(n-|z|^2)^2p_n=|z|^2$;

(d) the distribution is the Poisson distribution with parameter $\lambda=|z|^2$, and it is nontrivial for every $z\ne0$: $p_n>0$ for all $n$.
